  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$5.84B** of glass and glass products in 2025, down 4.2% from $6.10B in 2024 and below the 2023 peak of $6.15B. The two biggest groups are car parts and fiberglass: **rear-view mirrors for vehicles** (7009.10, **$1.59B**, mostly to Germany, Japan, Canada and Mexico) and **glass fibres** (7019, **$1.42B**: fiberglass rovings, yarn, mats, fabrics and glass-wool insulation, mostly to Mexico and Canada). Next come float glass (7005, $407M), glass bottles and jars (7010, $370M), quartz and other glass tubes and rods (7002, $295M), safety glass (7007, $288M) and laboratory glassware (7017, $256M). Canada (**29%**) and Mexico (**15%**) are the largest buyers. Exports are up **1.2%** in 2026 so far: $3.97B from January to August.\n\nTariffs bite in two places. **Canada** has charged a **50% surtax** on U.S. glass bottles, jars and other glass containers (7010.90) since September 8, 2026, even when they qualify for CUSMA; bottles used to package food or drinks in Canada can get it back. **China** adds a 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods to its 2018–2019 counter-tariffs, so U.S. rear-view mirrors pay **30%** and fiberglass about 28% unless the importer holds an exclusion. Everywhere else the main buyers charge little or nothing: Mexico and Korea 0% under their trade agreements, the EU 0% on all U.S. glass since July 1, 2026, Japan 0% on mirrors and fiberglass. For mirrors and car glass, the buyer's vehicle type approval matters more than the tariff.",
